import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(2,4,"Manas: Processing, Attention and Fluctuation","Once today, notice the radar switching, and give it one thing to rest on for a minute: a word, a breath or a posture.",[
mc("U","What is manas, according to Conversation 2.4?",
 ["The radar: the first-contact worker that forms notions and weighs alternatives, fast and restless.","The decider: the worker that settles what a thing is, what it means and what to do.","The memory-field: the store of impressions that colours what comes next in the mind.","The I-maker: the worker that stamps each experience as mine and draws a boundary."],0,
 "Manas is defined by saṅkalpa and vikalpa: forming notions and weighing alternatives, is it this or that, should I or shouldn’t I. It scans, samples, compares and flags.",
 "Manas is the radar","Part 1"),
mc("A","A woman says, “I am just an anxious, scattered person.” What does Conversation 2.4 say about this?",
 ["She is mistaking a scattered state of manas for a permanent fact about herself.","She is right, since a person’s attention is fixed from birth and cannot be changed.","She is wrong, since scanning is a defect and no scattered mind is ever natural.","She is right, as long as she accepts it and makes no attempt to alter her habits."],0,
 "Scanning is not a defect: it keeps us responsive and alive. The real distinction is between responsive flexibility, which sweeps when needed and then rests, and compulsive scattering, which cannot stop.",
 "A scattered state is not a permanent fact about you","Part 1"),
judge("“Scanning is a defect of the mind, and a healthy mind would not scan at all.”",F,
 "Scanning keeps us responsive and alive. The trouble is only the compulsive kind, which cannot stop. Healthy manas sweeps when needed, then rests.",
 "Scanning is not a defect; compulsive scattering is the trouble","Part 1"),
mc("A","A man’s phone buzzes all day, and by evening his mind is jumbled. How does Conversation 2.4 read this?",
 ["Near-continuous switching fragments the whole instrument, and his attention is the product.","His memory is failing, since a mind that is well would keep all of the day in order.","His judgement is weak, since a clear decider would simply ignore the phone altogether.","His Self is disturbed, since the awareness behind the mind has been scattered as well."],0,
 "Phones, feeds and notifications exploit attention deliberately. The result is near-continuous switching, which fragments the whole instrument.",
 "Constant switching fragments the instrument","Part 2"),
mc("A","A teacher feels that her crisis comes from existential doubt. Which cascade of Conversation 2.4 may be at work?",
 ["Scattered manas jumbles buddhi’s picture, so ahaṁkāra reacts and chitta stores the residue.","Strong chitta overrides manas, so that the past decides what she sees and feels now.","A weak ahaṁkāra gives way to buddhi, which then judges too slowly in every case.","A calm manas hands buddhi too little, so that no judgement can be made at all."],0,
 "Much mental suffering is attentional overload and not existential crisis, which is liberating, because the first fix is practical: steady the radar and the whole chain calms.",
 "Often it is attentional overload, not existential crisis","Part 2"),
mc("U","What does the science in Conversation 2.4 say about attention?",
 ["It is a limited resource, switching has a measurable cost, and focus must be trained.","It is unlimited, so a person can do many tasks at once without any cost at all.","It is fixed, so training makes no difference to how long a person can attend.","It is automatic, so a person has no say in what draws it and what does not."],0,
 "Multitasking is mostly an illusion. Novelty and threat hijack attention automatically, which is what feeds exploit. What you attend to is the world you end up living in.",
 "Attention is limited, and switching has a cost","Part 2"),
mc("A","A man wants a simple tool to steady his scattered mind. Which fits Conversation 2.4?",
 ["Japa, posture, slow breath and silence cut the switching load and give the radar one rhythm.","A long list of new tasks, since a very busy mind has less room left to scatter in.","A strong effort of will, since pressing the mind still is the quickest way to calm it.","A new phone, since a better device is the one thing that would solve the problem."],0,
 "These simple practices are precision tools aimed at manas. Japa gives the radar a single reference rhythm, and the restless scanning settles into one calm oscillation.",
 "Simple practices reduce the switching load","Part 3"),
mc("A","A woman presses her mind to be still, and it springs back harder. What does Conversation 2.4 suggest?",
 ["Not force but abhyāsa and vairāgya: patient return, and loosening the pull of objects.","More force, since the mind will give way if it is pressed for long enough each day.","Giving up, since a mind that springs back cannot be trained by any known practice.","Avoiding all objects, since the pull of the world is the whole of what is wrong."],0,
 "Forcing the mind still is like pressing water flat: it springs back. As the Gītā tells Arjuna, trained manas becomes responsive without being reactive.",
 "Abhyāsa and vairāgya, not force","Part 3"),
judge("“Trained manas becomes responsive without being reactive, and this is the layer that mindfulness works on.”",T,
 "Training is by abhyāsa, patient repeated practice, coming back gently again and again, and vairāgya, loosening the compulsive pull of objects. It is ancient diagnosis and modern confirmation.",
 "Trained manas is responsive without being reactive","Part 3"),
mc("A","A student asks why japa, a repeated word, helps when she is scattered. What does Conversation 2.4 say?",
 ["It gives the radar one reference rhythm, so the scanning settles into a single calm oscillation.","It blocks every thought, so that the mind is left empty and quite unable to scatter.","It tires the mind out, so that it gives up scanning because it has no energy left.","It pleases a deity, which is the whole of its effect whatever else may be going on."],0,
 "Japa, a repeated word or name, is one of the precision tools aimed at manas. In Module 3 this steadiness is read as the frequency fₘ, and everything downstream improves.",
 "Japa gives the radar one rhythm to lock onto","Part 3")])
